WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:08.291
Welcome to chalkcast. This short walkthrough shows you how to set up an assignment, and then what your students will see.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.712
Part one. Setting up an assignment.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:21.379
Start by opening Instructor setup from your chalkcast lesson. Bookmark the page so you can come back to it.

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:32.733
For each assignment, type a name, the problem you want students to teach, and your rubric, with one item on each line. Then choose a time limit.

00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:38.809
You can write your own problem, or press Choose from the question bank.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.196
Pick a unit and a competency. The bank starts with human anatomy.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:55.137
Each competency has multiple choice questions and draw to know it prompts. When you find one you like, press Use this question.

00:00:57.133 --> 00:01:01.295
The problem and a starter rubric fill in for you. You can edit both.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:10.789
Leave this box checked if your students will turn in their video through Canvas Studio. That way, the video gets captions.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.001
When you are ready, press Make links and instructions.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.988
Each assignment now has its own student link.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.553
Press Copy instructions, and paste them into the assignment in your course site. The link and the formatting come along with it.

00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:39.715
If a student has extended time, copy a time and a half link, or a double time link, and send it to that student.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:48.784
Before you leave, save your assignments to a file. That gives you a copy, and lets you load them on another computer.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:57.777
One more step, in Canvas. Set the assignment to accept file uploads, and allow more than one attempt.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:02.008
Part two. What your students do.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.119
Students open your link and press Start. They do not need an account.

00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:12.737
Your problem is already filled in for them.

00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:19.087
Next, they turn on their camera and microphone. Both are required.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:26.563
When they can see themselves, and the gold bar moves as they talk, they go to the whiteboard.

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:32.067
A banner reminds them that they are not recording yet.

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:35.668
They press Record.

00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:41.575
A short countdown gives them a moment to get ready.

00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:46.725
Then they draw, and explain their thinking out loud.

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:54.929
Their camera and their voice are recorded together with the drawing.

00:02:56.933 --> 00:03:05.745
The tools include a pen, a marker, an eraser, text, lines, arrows, boxes, and ovals, in several colors and sizes.

00:03:07.733 --> 00:03:11.916
They can also add pages, zoom in, undo, or add a picture.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.957
Your rubric sits beside the camera, so they can check off each item as they cover it.

00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:23.187
When they are done, they press Finish.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.804
They watch the video, and give it a name.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.743
Then they download the video.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.969
Next, they download a PDF of every whiteboard page.

00:03:39.967 --> 00:03:42.997
Last, they upload both files to your assignment.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.327
Part three. Assignment defense.

00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:59.105
An assignment defense lets you ask one student a follow-up question about work they already turned in. Type the assignment title and your question.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:07.693
Pick the recording time, and decide whether the student may use the whiteboard. Then press Make defense link.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:16.912
You get a link, and a message to send the student. The message explains what to expect. The question is not in it.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.275
You also get a record of the interaction to keep with your own files.

00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:37.599
For a whole class, scroll to Many defense links at once. Paste one question per line, or choose a file of questions. To track who gets which link, put initials or an ID first.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:49.649
Press Make all links. Each question gets its own link, with its own one attempt record. Copy the table into a spreadsheet, or download it as a file.

00:04:51.633 --> 00:04:56.072
The student checks the camera and microphone, then presses Begin Defense.

00:04:58.067 --> 00:05:06.217
The question appears, and recording starts at the same moment. The student gets one attempt, with no pausing or restarting.

00:05:08.233 --> 00:05:24.000
Copy and the right click menu are turned off on the question. If the student tries one during the recording, or presses a screenshot key, it is listed on the defense record. Keep in mind that a screenshot or a phone photo cannot be blocked.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:37.351
Two things to know. The one attempt rule is kept on the student's own device. And chalkcast cannot detect a second device, so treat the recording as one piece of evidence.

00:05:39.367 --> 00:05:45.320
That is everything. The instructor guide and the student guide are in this lesson if you need them.
